 \begin{abstract}
-We establish training-derived conditions that certify or exclude nearest-prototype agreement in Gaussian radial basis function classifiers over continuous bandwidth--penalty regions. A class-wise residual decomposition, prototype-block spectral floor, and exact penalty envelope certify complete regions $(0,t]\times[\lambda_0,\infty)$. A strict competing vote in the large-penalty limit supplies a finite disagreement bound, and a signed interval construction certifies local bandwidth bands with finite penalty floors. A frozen MNIST and Fashion-MNIST study spans five seeds and 50--200 prototypes. Immediately beyond the sufficient frontier, more than 99.5\% of query--region pairs remain unresolved. At the median-distance bandwidth scale, the original witness union excludes 88.10\% and 84.48\% of queries, respectively. Local bands at half this scale certify 16.88\% and 40.09\% at ten times the endpoint Gram-matrix norm. Matched-coverage class analysis reveals concentrated predictions within some certified subsets. Across all 30 dataset--seed--budget groups, 328 readouts retain at least 95\% of their group's best validation accuracy; all have zero full-width certificate coverage. Exhaustive reconstruction of 3,366,480 query--width paths identifies 190,405 returns to agreement after sampled disagreement. Expanded finite probes recover at least 95\% of the original limiting-witness cases after mean 15.6 and 20.2 checks at the median-distance scale. These results establish computable continuous decision-persistence guarantees and characterize their empirical separation from competitive prediction, finite-path behavior, and selected-set correctness.
+Gaussian radial basis function classifiers combine similarity responses from multiple prototypes, so their predictions can differ from the label of the nearest prototype. Determining when that label remains a valid summary as bandwidth and regularization change requires a guarantee over a family of fitted classifiers. This study derives such guarantees from the training data. A class-wise residual bound certifies agreement for every positive bandwidth up to a chosen endpoint and every ridge penalty at or above a chosen floor. Limiting class votes provide finite thresholds for disagreement and support a second certificate over local bandwidth bands. Evaluation on MNIST and Fashion-MNIST uses five seeds, three prototype budgets, and 2,880 archived readouts. With 100 prototypes, an endpoint defined by median prototype separation, and a zero penalty floor, the combined disagreement tests exclude full-region agreement for 88.10\% and 84.48\% of queries, respectively. Local bands admit additional certified regions at large penalties. All 328 readouts retaining at least 95\% of the best validation accuracy for their dataset, seed, and prototype budget have zero full-width certificate coverage. The finite penalty paths also reveal returns to agreement after disagreement, and class-conditioned comparisons identify concentrated predictions within certified subsets. These findings provide computable tests of whether proximity remains a valid label summary across a model family and characterize the relationship between guaranteed persistence, finite-parameter agreement, and predictive accuracy.
 \end{abstract}
