 \section{Introduction}
-The label of the nearest prototype does not always describe a Gaussian radial basis function (RBF) classifier's decision. Its learned output layer combines responses from all prototypes, with coefficients determined by the full labeled training sample. We establish training-derived conditions that determine when the nearest-prototype label persists throughout a continuous bandwidth--penalty region, when that persistence is impossible, and which queries remain unresolved. The proofs give both sufficient agreement conditions and finite disagreement thresholds. Experiments on MNIST and Fashion-MNIST show that sufficient certification disappears before widespread identified failures of the requested family, local width bands admit additional guarantees at large penalties, and all 328 archived readouts meeting the 95\% validation-competitive rule have zero full-width coverage. The analysis therefore locates both the guaranteed persistence regions and their empirical separation from high-accuracy operating points.
-
-A prototype is a reference input used to measure similarity. Here, prototypes are selected training images. Each Gaussian response decreases with distance from its prototype, and its bandwidth controls how broadly that response extends. Ridge regression learns the coefficients that combine these responses into class scores. The separation between nonlinear basis functions and linear output estimation is central to the RBF formulation of Broomhead and Lowe \citeyearpar{broomhead1988}. It also creates the present decision-summary problem: the largest individual response need not identify the class with the largest weighted score.
-
-Existing research establishes several relevant mechanisms. Coefficient-regularized RBF networks have a data-dependent kernel interpretation and statistical error bounds \citep{que2016}. Gaussian narrow-bandwidth limits have a nearest-neighbour dominance mechanism, also discussed in neural-network kernel analyses \citep{radhakrishnan2023}. Prototype architectures can impose a relation between components and predicted classes \citep{saralajew2025}, and learned Gaussian geometry can reveal feature importance \citep{dagostino2024}. Regularization-path methods bound optimization or validation behavior over parameter intervals \citep{shibagaki2015,ndiaye2019}. Our question concerns a fixed conventional ridge RBF family: which computable training conditions establish or rule out equality with a particular nearest-prototype label as both bandwidth and regularization vary?
-
-The parameter region specifies what the summary must survive. For a fixed query, the first region includes every positive bandwidth up to an upper endpoint and every finite ridge penalty above a floor. This asks whether proximity remains a valid label summary as responses broaden and regularization changes the readout. Large penalties expose a specific structural test: after positive rescaling, the class scores converge to training-label votes assembled through prototype features. A competing class that strictly wins this vote at any included width forces a finite disagreement inside the requested region. Thus an unbounded penalty interval supplies a way to distinguish failure of the family property from failure of a sufficient inequality.
-
-We first compare the fitted weights with weights that assign each prototype to its own class. An exact residual decomposition separates prototype overlap, non-prototype labeled responses, and a quadratic overlap correction. A spectral floor and a two-endpoint envelope then bound the effect of that residual throughout $(0,t]\times[\lambda_0,\infty)$. Comparing the bound with a query margin certifies agreement without fitting a readout. The large-penalty test complements this sufficient condition with an exclusion criterion and an explicit finite witness threshold. Their outputs classify each query and requested region as certified, excluded, or unresolved.
-
-The limiting votes also support a second positive construction. A signed interval enclosure controls their finite-penalty correction over a local bandwidth band $[s,t]$, producing a certificate for $[s,t]\times[\Lambda,\infty)$ with a finite floor $\Lambda$. Together, the results provide three analytical capabilities: full-width sufficient certification with ordered class-wise and norm comparators; strict exclusion of impossible full-width regions; and local-width penalty-tail certification. The frozen image study measures the first capability and its bandwidth frontier. Focused post hoc analyses measure the exclusion partition and local certificate on the same 100-prototype groups, reporting region geometry, coverage, fitted agreement, and class accuracy separately. Follow-up comparisons locate sufficient reversal bounds relative to validation-based competitive models, measure exact overlap with all archived finite witnesses, and compare certified subsets with informed selectors at identical coverage. Class-balanced selection diagnostics identify which queries and classes account for certified-subset accuracy. Exhaustive reconstruction of all 30 original groups then measures observed penalty-path reversals, validation-competitive coverage, and stability across prototype budgets and seeds. A fixed-order replay identifies how finite exclusions accumulate relative to limiting witnesses. Figure~\ref{fig:paths} introduces the two decisions whose equality is studied.
+A prototype gives a classifier a reference example against which to compare a new input. In a Gaussian radial basis function (RBF) network, distance to each prototype determines a similarity response, and a learned output layer combines those responses into class scores \citep{broomhead1988}. The nearest prototype supplies a simple candidate label for the input; the weighted combination supplies the network's prediction. This study investigates when those two labels agree throughout a range of bandwidths and ridge penalties, using mathematical guarantees and an evaluation on MNIST and Fashion-MNIST.
+
+The question arises when proximity to a labeled training example is used to summarize a fitted decision. Bandwidth determines how broadly each prototype responds, and the ridge penalty controls the coefficients learned from the labeled training sample. Changing either parameter can change the winning class. Agreement at one setting therefore leaves the validity of the same summary at other settings undetermined. A guarantee over a declared parameter region makes the scope of the summary explicit; a disagreement within that region identifies where the requested summary fails.
+
+Prior work supplies several parts of this problem. Srihari and colleagues \citeyearpar{srihari1979} derived conditions for nearest-neighbour and exponential-potential classifiers to have identical decision surfaces. Que and Belkin \citeyearpar{que2016} analyze coefficient-regularized RBF networks through data-dependent kernels and statistical error bounds. Saralajew and colleagues \citeyearpar{saralajew2025} directly examine the mismatch between nearby prototypes and weighted class decisions, then construct a network with an explicit prototype-class relationship. Parameter-range guarantees offer a complementary perspective: Shibagaki and colleagues \citeyearpar{shibagaki2015} and Ndiaye and colleagues \citeyearpar{ndiaye2019} bound validation behavior along regularization paths. These contributions motivate studying label agreement across a conventional RBF family in which both the Gaussian features and the ridge readout change.
+
+The objective is to determine, from fixed training data and selected labeled prototypes, which queries retain their nearest-prototype label throughout a specified bandwidth--penalty region. The analysis returns three possible conclusions. A certified region guarantees agreement for every parameter pair it contains. An excluded region contains a strict disagreement. A region receiving neither conclusion remains unresolved. Figure~\ref{fig:paths} shows the two label calculations whose equality defines this problem. This formulation connects the geometric reference to the complete fitted family and gives an explicit meaning to a certificate's acceptance or abstention.
+
+The first construction covers every positive bandwidth up to an endpoint and every ridge penalty at or above a floor. It compares learned weights with a reference that assigns each prototype to its own class. Training responses, grouped by class, bound the difference between the two sets of weights. A second calculation examines the class votes approached as the penalty grows. At a given bandwidth, a strictly opposing vote gives a finite threshold beyond which agreement fails. When the nearest-prototype class leads those votes throughout a local bandwidth band, a signed bound on the finite-penalty correction gives a further agreement certificate. The resulting full-width and local-band guarantees address two specified geometries of parameter variation.
+
+The empirical analysis uses five seeds and 50, 100, or 200 prototypes for each of two image datasets. It measures where the certificates apply, where strict disagreements exclude a requested region, and how these outcomes relate to fitted accuracy. The evidence reveals a separation between sufficient full-width coverage and competitive prediction: all 328 archived readouts retaining at least 95\% of their group's best validation accuracy have zero such coverage. Local bandwidth bands provide additional guarantees at large penalties. Exact witness overlaps, sampled penalty paths, and matched-coverage class analyses explain how these region-level findings relate to finite decisions and the composition of certified query subsets. Together, the proofs and measurements establish a training-derived analysis of nearest-prototype label persistence and identify its empirical reach in the studied model families.
 
